<section class="split-section research-project project-image-left">
    <div class="container">

            <div class="split-row">
                    <div class="media-content">

                            <p class="text-justify">
                                <strong>Optical Turbulence</strong><br>Optical turbulence is the study of the nonlinear interaction of light propagating through a nonlinear medium. This can include light propagating through single- and multi-mode optical fibres or through nonlinear liquid crystals. Principally, optical turbulence can be broken down into the study of weakly nonlinear wave interactions <strong><em>optical wave turbulence</em></strong> and the interaction of strongly nonlinear coherent structures in <strong><em>fully developed optical turbulence</em></strong>. My current research activity is focussed on the cross-over from optical wave turbulence to fully developed optical turbulence where the formation of coherent structures such as optical vortices (in defocussing media) and solitons (in focussing media) arise through nonlinear wave interactions.
                            </p>

                    </div>
                    <div class="figure">
                        <img src="/assets/images/optical_turbulence.png" alt="Optical Turbulence" width="438" height="214" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
                    </div>
